console.log(renderer.info.render);
// { frame: ..., calls: 87, triangles: 152340, points: 0, lines: 0 }
If calls ≫ 200 and the scene is laggy, the problem is the draw-call count, not GPU rasterisation. The fix is one of the patterns in this post.

InstancedMesh draws N copies of one geometry with one material in one draw call, each copy with its own 4×4 matrix (transform) and optionally its own colour.
This is the answer to "I have 10,000 trees / rocks / windows / grass blades / particles".
import * as THREE from 'three';
const COUNT = 5000;
const geo = new THREE.BoxGeometry(0.4, 0.4, 0.4);
const mat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x88aa66 });
const mesh = new THREE.InstancedMesh(geo, mat, COUNT);
const dummy = new THREE.Object3D(); // reusable for matrix math
for (let i = 0; i < COUNT; i++) {
dummy.position.set(
(Math.random() - 0.5) * 200,
0,
(Math.random() - 0.5) * 200
);
dummy.rotation.y = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
dummy.scale.setScalar(0.5 + Math.random() * 1.5);
dummy.updateMatrix();
mesh.setMatrixAt(i, dummy.matrix);
}
mesh.instanceMatrix.needsUpdate = true;
mesh.castShadow = true;
mesh.receiveShadow = true;
scene.add(mesh);
That entire scene = 1 draw call regardless of COUNT.

const color = new THREE.Color();
for (let i = 0; i < COUNT; i++) {
color.setHSL(Math.random(), 0.5, 0.6);
mesh.setColorAt(i, color);
}
mesh.instanceColor.needsUpdate = true;
The material respects per-instance colour automatically.

renderer.setAnimationLoop(() => {
const t = clock.getElapsedTime();
for (let i = 0; i < COUNT; i++) {
dummy.position.y = Math.sin(t + i * 0.1) * 0.5;
dummy.updateMatrix();
mesh.setMatrixAt(i, dummy.matrix);
}
mesh.instanceMatrix.needsUpdate = true;
renderer.render(scene, camera);
});
Reading/writing every instance every frame is fine up to ~10k instances on the main thread. For 100k+ instances, move the update to a Web Worker or use a vertex shader (Post 09) that computes positions from a base attribute + instance id.

const hits = raycaster.intersectObject(mesh);
if (hits.length) {
const i = hits[0].instanceId; // which instance was clicked
// hide it:
dummy.scale.setScalar(0);
dummy.updateMatrix();
mesh.setMatrixAt(i, dummy.matrix);
mesh.instanceMatrix.needsUpdate = true;
}
(Three.js doesn't have first-class per-instance visibility; scale = 0 is the common trick.)

InstancedMesh requires every instance to share the same geometry. BatchedMesh (added in r156) lifts that restriction — many different geometries can be rendered in one draw call as long as they share a material.
Use when: a city of unique buildings, a furniture catalogue, a forest with several tree species.
import * as THREE from 'three';
const SHARED = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0xcccccc });
const batch = new THREE.BatchedMesh(
/* maxInstanceCount */ 200,
/* maxVertexCount */ 200_000,
/* maxIndexCount */ 600_000,
SHARED
);
const cube = new THREE.BoxGeometry(1, 1, 1);
const cone = new THREE.ConeGeometry(0.5, 1, 16);
const torus = new THREE.TorusGeometry(0.5, 0.2, 12, 24);
const cubeId = batch.addGeometry(cube);
const coneId = batch.addGeometry(cone);
const torusId = batch.addGeometry(torus);
for (let i = 0; i < 200; i++) {
const id = batch.addInstance([cubeId, coneId, torusId][i % 3]);
const m = new THREE.Matrix4().compose(
new THREE.Vector3((i - 100) * 1.5, 0, 0),
new THREE.Quaternion(),
new THREE.Vector3(1, 1, 1)
);
batch.setMatrixAt(id, m);
}
scene.add(batch);
200 different-shape objects, 1 draw call. The original cube/cone/torus geometries are merged into the batch's internal buffer.

For static scenery where you don't need per-object updates, merge geometries into a single big mesh:
import { BufferGeometryUtils } from 'three/addons/utils/BufferGeometryUtils.js';
const pieces = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
const g = new THREE.BoxGeometry(1, 1, 1);
g.translate(Math.random() * 50, 0, Math.random() * 50);
pieces.push(g);
}
const merged = BufferGeometryUtils.mergeGeometries(pieces);
const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(merged, new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0xaaaaaa }));
scene.add(mesh);
pieces.forEach((g) => g.dispose());
1 draw call, fully baked. No per-object animation possible — to move one cube, you'd modify the whole merged geometry. Use for static decoration (walls, ground details, fixed environment).

const merged = BufferGeometryUtils.mergeGeometries(pieces, /* useGroups */ true);
const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(merged, [matA, matB, matC]);
Groups define which sub-range uses which material — still one geometry but multiple draw calls (one per group). Useful for merging objects that genuinely need different materials while keeping vertex data shared.
